WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.760
Today the issue becomes complicated. Chechnya or&nbsp;
Chechen Republic, has recalled the most important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:10.720
geopolitical problem for the Soviet Union&nbsp;
first and then for the Russian Federation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:16.160
We find ourselves here, in a territory located&nbsp;
on the northern flank of the Great Caucasus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:19.760
mostly flat and not very&nbsp;
rich in natural resources,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:24.960
bordering Georgia to the south and three other&nbsp;
autonomous republics of the Russian Federation:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:30.880
to the west with Ingushetia and North Ossetia, to&nbsp;
the east with Dagestan and to the northwest with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:36.000
the Russian kraj of Stavropol. It's not easy to&nbsp;
establish the productive potential of Chechnya,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:41.280
which has been almost wiped out by the destruction&nbsp;
of war as far as industrial plants are concerned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:46.240
and seriously weakened in the agricultural sector&nbsp;
as well. The main resource is the transit of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:52.240
the oil pipeline linking the Dagestan city of&nbsp;
Machackala and the city of Baku in Azerbaijan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:57.280
on the western shore of the Caspian Sea with&nbsp;
Novorossijsk on the Black Sea. Today's video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:02.000
is not intended to be a detailed reconstruction&nbsp;
of the contrasts, I would say almost secular,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:06.480
between this rebellious republic and Moscow,&nbsp;
because my friend Simone has already talked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:12.000
enough about it. My intent is rather to talk about&nbsp;
the rise of its current leader Ramzan Kadyrov,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:17.200
because even if officially Chechnya today&nbsp;
presents a facade of peace and reconstruction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:22.080
after the two conflicts that have opposed it to&nbsp;
the suffocating grip of neighboring Russia, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:27.520
tyranny of its ruler, established with the silent&nbsp;
blessing of the Kremlin, continues to eradicate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:32.480
even the mildest forms of dissent. Through the&nbsp;
law enforcement and security agencies under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:38.960
his control, Ramzan has used brutal methods of&nbsp;
repression (kidnappings, enforced disappearances,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:44.960
torture, extrajudicial executions, and collective&nbsp;
punishment practices) not only against suspected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:50.320
armed insurgents and their suspected collaborators&nbsp;
but also against other action groups deemed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:56.480
inconvenient, such as independent journalists,&nbsp;
Salafist Muslims, drug users, and those caught&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.480 --> 00:02:02.240
drunk driving. Let's take a few steps back. At the&nbsp;
dawn of the third millennium, after large-scale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:07.600
Russian military operations had brought Chechnya&nbsp;
back under federal rule, the Kremlin gradually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:13.280
began to hand over responsibility for governing&nbsp;
the republic and conducting counterinsurgency&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:18.960
operations to pro-Russian Chechen leaders. In&nbsp;
his 2000 speech to the Federal Assembly, Putin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:24.720
portrayed Chechnya as the supreme obstacle to be&nbsp;
overcome to hold the country together, saying:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:30.080
"Russia has received a fundamental challenge to&nbsp;
its sovereignty and territorial integrity. It has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:35.680
come face to face with a force striving to redraw&nbsp;
the geopolitical map of the world." The domination&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:40.480
of Chechnya would have been the cornerstone&nbsp;
of Putin's project of building a strong state,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:46.000
because it was the emblem of the Kremlin's control&nbsp;
over its subordinates. Putin's administration,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:51.360
through collaboration with the Kadyrov family, set&nbsp;
up the so-called policy of Chechenization, that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:56.880
changing the nature of the conflict from "Russians&nbsp;
against Chechens" to "good Chechens against rebel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:01.520
Chechens". And it's likely that the Russian&nbsp;
president, in order to reinforce his statist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:07.440
vision and end the ongoing conflict, needed to&nbsp;
find a local lord with whom the local population&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:12.080
could identify and who knew the intricate&nbsp;
traditions of the country. It was necessary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:16.960
to appoint as president someone who had a past in&nbsp;
the ranks of the insurrectionists and therefore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:22.960
enjoyed a good popular support. Akhmad Kadyrov's&nbsp;
past seemed a guarantee in every aspect. He was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:28.720
a Sufi Muslim who didn't identify himself with&nbsp;
the Salafists (the radicals) but who ironically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:34.400
after becoming Grand Mufti in 1995, that is&nbsp;
the highest religious authority in Chechnya,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:39.520
accepted to bless the jihad against the Russian&nbsp;
state, siding with the separatist wing, led by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:45.280
the then president Dzokhar Dudaev. Later, the&nbsp;
Salafists criticized him for being too lenient,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:51.120
which was unacceptable in their "project of real&nbsp;
and pure Islam." As a result, a conflict developed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:56.880
between Sufi and Salafist Muslims, and eventually&nbsp;
Akhmad chose to align himself with the Russian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:02.480
leadership and strengthen Sufi rule in Chechnya.&nbsp;
Unfortunately for the Kremlin, Akhmad was not an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:07.760
efficient puppet: he was despised as a corrupt&nbsp;
and cold-hearted politician who argued with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:13.360
political partners and civil administrators that&nbsp;
Moscow entrusted to him. He preferred to rely on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:19.120
his relatives and his own security force, known&nbsp;
as the SB (slushva besopasnosti ) or kadyrovtsky,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:24.960
led by his son Ramzan. In the meantime, the second&nbsp;
Chechen war raged and the Chechenization policy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:30.160
didn't seem to bear fruit, as rebel cells sprouted&nbsp;
up all over the country, flooding the media&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:36.080
with news of instability related to the small&nbsp;
Caucasian region. The climax came in October 2002&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:41.520
when a commando of rebels in the service of the&nbsp;
insurrectionist leader Shamil Basayev, besieged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:47.440
the Dubrovka theater in Moscow and held more than&nbsp;
800 people hostage, demanding the withdrawal of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:52.400
Russian forces from Chechnya and the end of&nbsp;
the conflict. The subsequent raid by Russian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:59.200
special forces led to the death of about 130&nbsp;
hostages, almost all of them suffocated by gas.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:04.480
Shamil, named after the famous warrior Imam Shamil&nbsp;
who led the resistance of the mountain tribes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:10.320
against the tsarist armies in the 19th century,&nbsp;
claimed responsibility for numerous attacks:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:16.000
after an incursion into neighboring Dagestan&nbsp;
in 1999, he repeatedly threatened to attack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:20.960
Russian cities, even boasting that he had an&nbsp;
army of "black widows," Chechen female kamikaze;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:27.120
his organization planted a bomb in the Moscow&nbsp;
subway on the morning of 02/06/2004, killing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:33.600
41 people. Finally, Akhmad, judged a traitor,&nbsp;
also fell victim to Basayev's murderous rampage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:39.680
when on 05/09/2004, two days after receiving his&nbsp;
second term as president, a bomb exploded at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:44.320
stadium in Grozny, where the Chechen leader&nbsp;
and a number of senior Chechen and Russian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.320 --> 00:05:50.000
officials were attending the World War II victory&nbsp;
day celebration. Blind to the growing threat, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:55.600
Kremlin continued with its Chechnya policy. While&nbsp;
waiting for Ramzan, still too young, to reach the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.600 --> 00:06:01.600
minimum age of 30 years to govern, it designated&nbsp;
Alu Alkhanov as Akhmad's successor. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:07.200
he had no popular base, lacking the charisma and&nbsp;
influence that had distinguished his predecessor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:12.080
surrounded by many questions about his real&nbsp;
ability to manage a region devastated by war&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:17.440
and to convince the Chechen rebels to lay down&nbsp;
their arms. His election took place the weekend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:22.400
before the event that triggered the decisive&nbsp;
change in Putin's approach to the Chechen issue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:29.040
namely the hostage crisis at the Beslan school&nbsp;
in North Ossetia on 09/01/2004 On that day more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:35.040
than 330 people lost their lives, many of them&nbsp;
children, and Putin had to open his eyes to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:40.960
obvious failure of his policy. 4 years later, in a&nbsp;
speech Putin stated that he had underestimated the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:46.640
complexity and the danger of the processes taking&nbsp;
place in Chechnya, failing to react adequately and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.640 --> 00:06:52.240
showing weakness. It was necessary, therefore,&nbsp;
to create a new system of forces and means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:57.680
to exercise control over the situation in the&nbsp;
North Caucasus. Concepts fully embodied by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.680 --> 00:07:02.800
protagonist of our video, Ramzan Kadyrov. He&nbsp;
is the master of Chechnya, a man who, contrary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.800 --> 00:07:08.960
to expectations, has managed to monopolize power&nbsp;
over the Chechen security forces in the territory.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:14.320
Still, it was hard to expect this from a man who&nbsp;
appeared in audiences before Putin dressed in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:19.840
training suit and who, given his poor education,&nbsp;
made every public speech a shameful disaster. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.840 --> 00:07:25.040
spite of this, Kadyrov knew how to operate within&nbsp;
the network of the numerous Chechen teips (the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:30.880
clans) and balance power among them perfectly,&nbsp;
which explains much of his strategic success and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:36.640
popularity in the eyes of his fellow citizens.&nbsp;
The Chechens are in fact a complex society,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.640 --> 00:07:42.560
based on extended families, the "teip" in fact,&nbsp;
and tradition dictates that the omnipotence of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:48.480
a single teip is unthinkable, because power must&nbsp;
be distributed equally among the various clans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:53.920
according to a horizontal structure of government.&nbsp;
This is precisely the reason that led to the fall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.920 --> 00:07:59.360
of President Akhmad, who probably knew his time&nbsp;
was running out when he affirmed the supremacy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:04.560
of his clan without consolidating it, having to&nbsp;
look more to his old comrades in arms than to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:09.840
the neighboring Russian giant. But Kadyrov junior&nbsp;
didn't repeat his father's mistakes and, enjoying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:15.520
the support of the Kremlin, he was able to spend&nbsp;
his resources to concentrate on internal affairs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:20.720
taking all the risks involved and eliminating&nbsp;
potential rivals. The centralization of power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:26.080
required first and foremost to get rid of anyone&nbsp;
who dared to challenge his military authority,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:31.840
especially influential warlords such as members&nbsp;
of the Yamadayev family, Said-Magomed Kakiev&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:37.600
and Movladi Baisarov. The Yamadayev brothers and&nbsp;
Kakiev commanded the Vostok and Zapad battalions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:43.040
respectively, each about 900 men strong and&nbsp;
the only ethnic Chechens in the Russian Defense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.040 --> 00:08:48.800
Ministry structure that weren't under Chechen&nbsp;
government control. Zapad, formed in 2003, was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.800 --> 00:08:55.920
pro-Russian and, at the time of the first Chechen&nbsp;
war in 1994 -1996, opposed separatist leader&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.920 --> 00:09:01.520
Dudaev. On the contrary, the Vostok was formed&nbsp;
by former rebels who passed to the Russian side&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.520 --> 00:09:06.800
during the second military campaign and thanks to&nbsp;
their loyalty and efficiency on the field, for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.800 --> 00:09:12.160
first time in the modern history of Russia, three&nbsp;
brothers of the same family (the Yamadaevs) were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.160 --> 00:09:17.920
decorated with medals for bravery, thus enjoying&nbsp;
not only the admiration of Moscow for the courage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.920 --> 00:09:23.280
shown in battle but also the control of the&nbsp;
commercial center of Gudermes, a city that lies on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.280 --> 00:09:29.360
the railway junction between Rostov on Don, Baku,&nbsp;
Astrakhan and Mozdok. There has never been good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.360 --> 00:09:35.280
blood between the Yamadaev family and the Kadyrov&nbsp;
family, and animosity exploded in April 2008&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.280 --> 00:09:41.040
when fighters of the Vostok battalion engaged in a&nbsp;
shootout with members of Ramzan's personal guard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:46.960
resulting in several deaths. In retaliation, the&nbsp;
Yamadaevs' older brother Ruslan, a former Duma&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.960 --> 00:09:52.560
deputy, was killed in Moscow five months later.&nbsp;
Sulim, commander of the battalion and military&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.560 --> 00:09:58.560
pride of the family, managed to escape from&nbsp;
Russia and settled in Dubai but in March 2009&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.560 --> 00:10:04.320
Adam Delimkhanov, Ramzan's right hand man, had him&nbsp;
assassinated. Kakiev instead, head of the Zapad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.320 --> 00:10:10.000
battalion then abolished in 2008, was the only one&nbsp;
to survive, because he never questioned Ramzan's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:15.520
power. Finally, Movladi Baisarov had been the&nbsp;
head of Akhmad's personal security service and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.520 --> 00:10:21.360
after his death in 2004, he set up the Gorets&nbsp;
security unit on his own, directly reporting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.360 --> 00:10:25.120
to the FSB (the Federal Security Service).&nbsp;
But Baisarov was one of those who liked to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.120 --> 00:10:31.680
play with fire, since he openly criticized Ramzan&nbsp;
several times, until he released in October 2006&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.680 --> 00:10:36.880
an interview to Moscow News in which he defined&nbsp;
him as a "medieval tyrant". The reaction of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.880 --> 00:10:41.920
Chechen leader was not long in coming and in fact&nbsp;
the following November, Baisarov was killed in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.920 --> 00:10:47.600
Moscow by a team of Chechen policemen, the Gorets&nbsp;
unit disbanded and its fighters reintegrated in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.600 --> 00:10:52.960
various units of the Chechen Ministry of Interior.&nbsp;
Once the main rival forces were defeated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.960 --> 00:10:58.160
the way was clear for Kadyrov. He was able to&nbsp;
monopolize the sphere of administration in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.160 --> 00:11:03.520
small republic, and Moscow is not only uninvolved&nbsp;
in Chechen internal affairs but receives no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.520 --> 00:11:08.720
information about the real state of affairs. One&nbsp;
could almost say that "what happens in Chechnya,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.720 --> 00:11:13.360
stays in Chechnya" and the fact that the&nbsp;
circumstances of Natalia Estemirova's murder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.360 --> 00:11:19.440
in 2009 are still unknown is a concrete proof of&nbsp;
that. Estemirova, a Russian activist who worked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.440 --> 00:11:25.040
with investigative journalist Anna Politkovskaia&nbsp;
and human rights lawyer Stanislav Markelov,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.040 --> 00:11:31.040
both of whom murdered in 2006 and 2009&nbsp;
respectively, documented hundreds of cases of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.040 --> 00:11:36.400
abuses in Chechnya by government security forces.&nbsp;
She carried out her research for the human rights&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.400 --> 00:11:42.080
organization "Memorial" and was therefore branded&nbsp;
as an undesired person in the eyes of Ramzan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.080 --> 00:11:48.560
who repeatedly stressed his willingness to silence&nbsp;
her forever. So that morning of 07/15/2009, when,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.560 --> 00:11:54.080
according to the Moscow office of Human Rights&nbsp;
Watch, Estemirova was kidnapped near her home in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.080 --> 00:11:59.600
Grozny and then found dead in a wooded area near&nbsp;
the village of Gazy-Yurt in Ingushetia, there was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.600 --> 00:12:04.880
little doubt as to who was behind the kidnapping.&nbsp;
The problem is that there has never been a tough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.880 --> 00:12:10.560
stance against Ramzan from the Kremlin. In&nbsp;
fact, even though Vice-President Dmitry Medvedev&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.560 --> 00:12:15.920
"expressed indignation at the murder and ordered&nbsp;
to take all necessary measures to carry out the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.920 --> 00:12:21.200
investigation" he always rejected all hypotheses&nbsp;
about Kadyrov's involvement in the crime.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.840 --> 00:12:54.720
After all, there is a dark power pact between&nbsp;
the federal government and the small republic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.720 --> 00:12:59.840
that mustn t be revealed to the outside world:&nbsp;
Ramzan was charged with creating the illusion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.840 --> 00:13:04.640
of a peaceful and flourishing Chechnya by&nbsp;
any means necessary, which explains his use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.640 --> 00:13:10.400
of violence outside Moscow's control. In the&nbsp;
2016 elections, Ramzan confirmed his role as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.400 --> 00:13:16.000
the undisputed leader with almost 98% (97.94%)&nbsp;
of votes in favor and from that moment until&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.000 --> 00:13:21.600
the present day he has continued his war against&nbsp;
political opponents and insurgents. But why was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.600 --> 00:13:26.800
such a high degree of consensus reached? Simple,&nbsp;
because in the months leading up to the vote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.800 --> 00:13:32.080
the authorities undertook a fierce crackdown&nbsp;
on anyone who thought total loyalty to Kadyrov&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.080 --> 00:13:38.080
was questionable. Among them were ordinary people&nbsp;
expressing dissent through social media; Russian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.080 --> 00:13:43.920
journalists like Boris Nemtsov, a critic of the&nbsp;
Chechen leadership who was assassinated in Moscow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.920 --> 00:13:49.120
on February 27, 2015; and the very few human&nbsp;
rights defenders who dared to challenge abuses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.120 --> 00:13:55.040
of power by Chechen law enforcement and security&nbsp;
agencies. A recent report describes contemporary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.040 --> 00:14:00.880
Chechnya as a "totalitarian state within a state,"&nbsp;
characterized by Kadyrov's interference in nearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.880 --> 00:14:06.720
every aspect of social life, including politics,&nbsp;
religion, academic discourse, and family matters.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.720 --> 00:14:11.440
Many experts describe his orders as being,&nbsp;
in practice, the only law in the republic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.440 --> 00:14:16.320
thanks to the cult that has been created around&nbsp;
his figure. The main driving force behind this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.320 --> 00:14:22.560
cult is Grozny TV and state radio, whose news&nbsp;
and current affairs programs often depict Kadyrov&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.560 --> 00:14:27.760
while also blaming local high officials for their&nbsp;
mistakes. It s well known his intensive use of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.760 --> 00:14:32.160
social media to set his public agenda, demand&nbsp;
obedience, identify and smear enemies. think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.160 --> 00:14:38.240
that his instagram account, which he launched in&nbsp;
February 2013, before being banned by Zuckerberg's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.240 --> 00:14:44.160
company, had about 1 million followers in 2015.&nbsp;
In more recent times, despite Ramzan professing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.160 --> 00:14:49.760
his loyalty to Vladimir Putin himself, his&nbsp;
insistence on having a free hand in Chechnya has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.760 --> 00:14:55.600
apparently begun to test the Kremlin's patience.&nbsp;
Moscow has condemned, albeit in mild terms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.600 --> 00:15:00.240
some of his more outrageous actions, such&nbsp;
as his public efforts to destroy the homes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.240 --> 00:15:05.760
of alleged rebels and the numerous fires that&nbsp;
followed, saying that no one, including Kadyrov,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.760 --> 00:15:11.040
has the right to impose extrajudicial punishment.&nbsp;
But these are just words and it is difficult to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.040 --> 00:15:16.160
believe that in the near future the situation will&nbsp;
change, also because Moscow needs someone like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.160 --> 00:15:21.920
Kadyrov to keep at bay a territory where tensions&nbsp;
are dormant, ready to explode again. Well, we are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.920 --> 00:15:26.960
done. I hope I have kept your attention up to the&nbsp;
end and I'll see you in the next video. Ciao!

